# H5000M runtime configuration. Logging is deliberately UCI-driven rather than baked in:
# diagnosing a modem fault should never require rebuilding and reflashing firmware, least
# of all on a device being administered remotely.
#
# level: error | warn | info | debug | trace
#   error  the thing failed and stays failed
#   warn   degraded, self-correcting, or retrying
#   info   state transitions worth keeping (activated, published, bearer lost)   [default]
#   debug  decisions and branches - which APN ladder rung, which candidate port
#   trace  the AT wire itself: every command and response
#
# Per-component keys override the global level. Components: fm350 (dialer),
# modem_atd (AT layer), sms, esim. Leave empty to inherit.
#
#   uci set h5000m.logging.fm350=trace; uci commit h5000m
#
# ⚠️ Levels are read ONCE when a process starts. Short-lived tools (atq, h5000m-sms) pick
# up a change on their next run, but a LONG-LIVED process does not: to raise the dialer's
# level you must restart it - `ifdown cellular; ifup cellular` - or it keeps the level it
# started with. No reboot and no rebuild, but not zero-touch either.
#
# ⚠️ A process logs under ONE component. The dialer sources the AT layer, so inside the
# dialer everything - including AT traces - is governed by `fm350`, and `modem_atd` there
# has no effect. `modem_atd` governs the AT layer in its own tools (atq, at-lease).

config logging 'logging'
	option level 'info'
	option fm350 ''
	option modem_atd ''
	option sms ''
	option esim ''
	# Identifier masking. AT traffic carries IMSI, ICCID, EID, phone numbers and whole SMS
	# bodies; syslog can be forwarded off-box. Applies at EVERY level, not just trace -
	# an ICCID in a warning is exactly as sensitive as one in a trace. On by default;
	# turning it off logs a warning. Set it back when you are done.
	option trace_redact '1'
	# Identical CONSECUTIVE messages collapse into one line plus "(repeated N times)".
	# The run is flushed when a different message is logged, or at this count - the cap
	# exists so a long-lived process cannot sit forever on an un-emitted counter.
	# This is a repeat COUNT, not a time window: taking a timestamp per line would cost a
	# fork per log call, which is the thing this library is built to avoid.
	option dedup_max '50'
